1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760
In November 1975, a massive cargo ship called the Edmund Fitzgerald was crossing

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,200
Lake Superior.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:12,880
On board were 29 people and more than 26,000 tons of iron ore.

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:16,320
The next day, the ship disappeared during a storm.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:18,320
No one heard a distress call.

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,320
And just minutes before it is believed to have gone down,

7
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:24,720
the crew was still answering radio calls from the bridge.

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:29,040
What happened between that final conversation and the ship's disappearance remains

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,120
unknown to this day.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,880
The Edmund Fitzgerald left the port of Superior on November 9.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,960
Its destination was the Detroit area, where it was supposed to deliver its cargo

12
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:41,080
after crossing Lake Superior.

13
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:45,760
For a ship more than 200 meters long, this was another routine voyage across the

14
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,000
Great Lakes.

15
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,800
Another freighter, the Arthur M.

16
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,800
Anderson, was heading in the same direction.

17
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,613
The two ships stayed in radio contact.

18
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:59,080
As the weather began to deteriorate, both changed course toward the Canadian shore,

19
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,027
hoping it would offer some protection from the waves.

20
00:01:02,053 --> 00:01:05,320
But on November 10, the storm grew stronger.

21
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:09,760
The wind shifted direction, and enormous waves built up on the eastern side of the

22
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:10,680
lake.

23
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,840
Snow reduced visibility.

24
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,400
Both ships were now trying to reach the more sheltered waters of Whitefish Bay.

25
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,600
That afternoon, the Fitzgerald reported damage.

26
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:24,320
Part of its deck railing had been damaged, there were problems with its ventilation

27
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,400
equipment, and the ship had developed a list.

28
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:29,040
Its pumps were running.

29
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,560
Later, its radars failed.

30
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,320
The Fitzgerald was sailing ahead, while the Anderson followed behind,

31
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:39,280
helping over the radio by reporting the ship's position relative to the shore and

32
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,680
other vessels.

33
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:45,120
That evening, the Anderson contacted the crew again and asked how they were managing

34
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,440
their problems.

35
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,840
The reply from the Fitzgerald was simple:

36
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,720
“We are holding our own.”

37
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:53,640
That was the last known radio contact.

38
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:58,040
Soon afterward, the Fitzgerald's radar echo disappeared from the screen.

39
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:01,480
When visibility improved, its lights were gone too.

40
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:04,293
Repeated radio calls went unanswered.

41
00:02:04,347 --> 00:02:07,400
The Anderson reported the disappearance to the Coast Guard.

42
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:11,840
Then, after reaching sheltered waters, the freighter turned around and headed back

43
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:14,200
into the storm to help with the search.

44
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,680
Lifeboats and other debris were found.

45
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:18,560
There were no survivors.

46
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,760
All 29 people aboard the Edmund Fitzgerald had died.

47
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,133
Later, search equipment located the wreck at a depth of about 160 meters.

48
00:02:28,187 --> 00:02:30,320
The ship lay in two main sections.

49
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,880
The bow was upright, while the stern was upside down.

50
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,080
But even the discovery of the wreck did not provide a final answer to the central

51
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:39,280
question:

52
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,160
what exactly happened in those last few minutes?

53
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:46,320
The United States Coast Guard concluded that water had probably been entering the

54
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:51,040
cargo hold gradually through hatch covers that were not sufficiently watertight.

55
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,520
As more water entered, the ship settled lower and lower in the lake.

56
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:59,520
The National Transportation Safety Board offered a different explanation for the

57
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:01,040
final stage of the disaster.

58
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:05,680
In its theory, water may also have been building up inside the ship.

59
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,800
Then one or more hatch covers gave way under water pressure.

60
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,520
As a result, the cargo hold flooded rapidly.

61
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:18,000
These were the conclusions of investigations, not a record of the ship's final

62
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:18,840
moments.

63
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,440
No one from the Fitzgerald returned to explain what happened.

64
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:25,760
We know the route it took, its final radio messages,

65
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,600
and where the wreck lies today.

66
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:32,160
But between the words “We are holding our own” and the loss of the ship,

67
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:35,440
there is still a gap that has never been fully reconstructed.
